# Fingerprints for gitleaks findings that are deliberately not secrets.
#
# Format: <commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>
#
# THIS FILE IS EMPTY ON PURPOSE. Read before adding to it.
#
# A fingerprint is pinned to a COMMIT SHA, so it is only ever valid for a
# commit that will never be rewritten. This repo merges squash-only
# (`gh api repos/BirdoVPN/Mobile-Client` reports allow_merge_commit=false,
# allow_rebase_merge=false, allow_squash_merge=true), so every commit on a PR
# branch is replaced by a NEW sha on main. A fingerprint added here for a
# branch commit is green on the PR and stops matching the instant the PR is
# squashed -- and .github/workflows/secret-scan.yml, which runs on push to main
# with fetch-depth: 0, then goes red ON MAIN, after approval, where nobody is
# watching.
#
# That is not hypothetical. This file used to carry:
#
#   * the same BirdoPQManager.swift:generic-api-key:52 finding twice, under two
#     different shas -- once per commit that touched the file;
#   * three PQ-fixture fingerprints pinned to a branch commit that would not
#     have survived this PR's own squash;
#   * scripts/capture-screenshots.ps1:generic-api-key:12 pinned to
#     32a2d5fbb2ac0e6438ad195db53900ddd7534343, a commit that is no longer an
#     ancestor of main at all. That entry had been dead for months and nobody
#     could tell, because a fingerprint that matches nothing looks exactly like
#     a fingerprint that is doing its job.
#
# All of those are now path/regex allowlists in .gitleaks.toml, which are
# sha-independent: they survive a squash, a rebase, and the line moving.
#
# scripts/check_gitleaksignore.sh fails the build if any fingerprint here is
# pinned to a commit that is not an ancestor of main.
